# November 28, 2026: a fifth Saskatchewan batch. Thirteen places spread across the province, picked from Wikipedia's lists
# of historic places in Saskatchewan, Regina, Battleford and Prince Albert, each read in the Canadian Register of Historic
# Places (historicplaces.ca): the east (Arcola, Vibank), the south (Willow Bunch, Val Marie, Ponteix), the centre and Regina
# (Watson, Regina), the northwest (Battleford, Cut Knife Hill) and the north (Duck Lake, Prince Albert). Owners and opening
# times that no page read here gave are marked "not checked".
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'sk_hdtm','label':'Saskatchewan: convents, courthouses and a ruined Government House','added':'2026-11-28 16:42'}
PLACES=[
N('arcola-court-house','Arcola Court House','Arcola','sk','civic','standing',49.63534,-102.48299,'exact','view','drive','','1908–1909',
  'A brick-faced court house built in 1908 and 1909, when the town expected to grow into a large centre and built on a scale like the courthouses of Saskatoon and Battleford. The court has long stopped sitting here, and the building has since housed several businesses. The town made it a municipal heritage property in 1986.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey brick-faced building by the Regina architects Storey and Van Egmond, in a quiet Edwardian Classical style: windows set in a symmetrical pattern and a projecting entrance with columns, topped by the provincial crest. The register calls it a simpler version of the Battleford court house of about 1907. Interior not checked.',
  'A prairie court house built for a boom that never came.',
  'At 619 Souris Avenue, Arcola. Present use and owner not checked; look from the street. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Arcola Court House on Souris Avenue',7504)],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=None,thn=None,hours='',
  arch='Storey and Van Egmond',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places: designed by the Regina firm of Storey and Van Egmond'),
N('vibank-convent','Vibank Convent','Vibank','sk','worship','standing',50.33497,-103.94736,'site','view','drive','','1923–',
  'The Ursuline sisters opened their first convent in Vibank in 1919. After a fire at their convent in Grayson, the order moved the province\'s only Ursuline motherhouse here, and this building was finished in 1923 as a convent, a girls\' school and a dormitory. It stayed the motherhouse until 1953 and a school and convent until 1977. The village made it a heritage property in 1992.',
  'A two-storey brick building, large and plain, with religious carvings around the entrance, a good deal of wood trim inside and pink stained-glass windows in the sanctuary, according to the register. The grounds were laid out for quiet walking, with lawns, concrete paths, statues and a small shrine, and a belt of conifers around them. What is inside now was not checked.',
  'A 1923 brick convent with pink glass in its chapel.',
  'At 101 2nd Avenue, Vibank. Present use and opening times were not found; look from the street. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Vibank Convent',1780)],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('willow-bunch-convent','The Convent (Willow Bunch Museum)','Willow Bunch','sk','civic','standing',49.38946,-105.63601,'site','open','drive','','1914–1921',
  'A three-storey convent built between 1914 and 1921 for the Sisters of the Cross, brought to Willow Bunch by local families who wanted a better school and French-language teaching. The sisters left in 1983, and the building then became the town museum and a French cultural centre. The town made it a heritage property in 1986.',
  'A stucco-clad building on a fieldstone foundation, with a mansard roof, dormers and a tower with a pyramid roof and a metal fence along its peak, all borrowed from the Second Empire style used by the Catholic church in Quebec. A niche with a statue is set into the tower. The main floor kept its classroom layout. What the museum shows was not checked.',
  'A mansard-roofed convent school that is now the town museum.',
  'The register names the present use as the local museum and a French cultural centre. Opening times were not found: ask in the town. Parking on the street (not checked on site). ',
  [HP('The Convent, Willow Bunch',3782)],conf='mixed',brick='no',bcol='none',th=None,thn=None,hours='?'),
N('val-marie-red-brick-school','The Red Brick School, Val Marie','Val Marie','sk','civic','standing',49.24486,-107.73192,'exact','open','drive','','1927–1985',
  'A two-room brick schoolhouse built in 1927, a year after Val Marie became a village. It was designed and built by the Waterman-Waterbury Company, a school supply firm whose plain square and rectangular plans became the model for many one- and two-room schools in the province. Some classes moved to a nearby convent in 1939, a third classroom was opened in the basement in 1953, and the school closed in 1985. It is now a museum of local history.',
  'A simple hip-roofed building with a central entrance of double wooden doors, multi-pane double-hung windows in their original wood casings, shake shingles, wood floors and mouldings, and painted plaster walls and ceilings, which the register lists as the parts worth keeping. What the museum displays was not checked.',
  'A 1927 two-room brick school, now the village museum.',
  'At 100 Centre Street, Val Marie, a village heritage property. The register says it serves as a local history museum; opening times were not found, so ring ahead or ask in the village. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('The Red Brick School',4149)],conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',th=None,thn=None,hours='?'),
N('ponteix-notre-dame-auvergne','Notre Dame d\'Auvergne Parish Church, Ponteix','Ponteix','sk','worship','standing',49.74689,-107.49256,'exact','view','drive','','1928–1930',
  'A very large Roman Catholic church built between 1928 and 1930 under Father Napoleon Poirier for a parish that French and Belgian settlers founded in 1908. It is one of the largest and most impressive churches in Saskatchewan. The town made it a municipal heritage property in 2005.',
  'Montreal architect Roland Simard of Larose and Simard planned it in a Romanesque Revival style drawn from northern French churches of the 12th century. It is clad in textured brick from Estevan, with twin square towers whose spires reach 34 metres, brick corbelling under the roof, round-arched windows and slender columns at the door. Inside, one open span 54 metres long and over 15 metres high seats 1,000 people. It is also one of the largest reinforced-concrete structures in the southwest of the province. Beside it stand the parish hall (1923), the Sisters of Notre Dame convent (1916) and the former Gabriel Hospital (1918).',
  'A cathedral-sized brick church built by a small prairie parish.',
  'At 317 2nd Street West, Ponteix. A working parish church; service times were not found, so ask in the town before counting on going in. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Notre Dame d\'Auvergne Parish Church',8399)],conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',th=None,thn=None,hours='',
  arch='Larose and Simard',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places: planned by Roland Simard of the Montreal firm Larose and Simard'),
N('watson-bank-of-commerce','Canadian Bank of Commerce, Watson (Watson and District Heritage Museum)','Watson','sk','civic','standing',52.12909,-104.52392,'exact','open','drive','','1906–1907',
  'A two-storey wooden bank built in 1906 and 1907 for the Canadian Bank of Commerce. It was made in sections in Vancouver by British Columbia Mills, Timber and Trading, shipped by rail and put up in a few days. Toronto bank architects Darling and Pearson drew three standard designs, and this is the largest of them and the best preserved of the prefabricated bank buildings the bank put up in railway towns across the prairies. It became a national historic site in 1976 and has housed the Watson and District Heritage Museum since July 1980.',
  'Neoclassical, in wood made to look like the stone and brick banks of bigger towns: a front gable with a pediment and bull\'s-eye window, a heavy dentilled cornice, fluted pilasters and a broken pediment over the door, in a pale colour scheme. Parts of the original banking layout survive, with living rooms upstairs for the bank staff.',
  'A bank that arrived by train in sections and was put up in days.',
  'At 201 Main Street, Watson, now the town\'s museum. Days and hours were not found on a page read for this entry: ring ahead. Parking on Main Street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Canadian Bank of Commerce National Historic Site of Canada',7564)],conf='mixed',brick='no',bcol='none',th=None,thn=None,hours='?',
  arch='Darling and Pearson',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places: designed by the Toronto bank architects Darling and Pearson'),
N('rcmp-depot-chapel-regina','RCMP Depot Chapel (Building 13), Regina','Regina','sk','worship','standing',50.45017,-104.66267,'site','ask','drive','','1883–',
  'Built in 1883 as a mess hall for the new Regina headquarters of the North West Mounted Police and turned into a chapel in 1895. The register calls it the only building left from the wooden barracks put up in 1882 and 1883, and the oldest known building still standing in Regina. It belongs to the RCMP Depot Division, the force\'s training centre.',
  'A long wooden building with a gable roof and a small steepled tower on its north end, plain outside, with an arched door in the middle of the front and tall pointed windows set at regular spaces. Inside, the register describes a restored, humble room with rustic wooden pews, dedicated stained-glass windows and many memorial plaques on the walls.',
  'The oldest building in Regina, a Mounted Police mess hall made into a chapel.',
  'It stands inside the working RCMP training academy on Dewdney Avenue West, which is not open ground: the register gives no visiting times and none were found, so ask the RCMP Depot about a visit. Do not walk in.',
  [HP('Royal Canadian Mounted Police Depot, Chapel',15982)],conf='mixed',brick='no',bcol='none',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('government-house-battleford-ruins','Government House ruins, Battleford','Battleford','sk','landmark','ruins',52.7118,-108.3084,'site','view','walk','','1876–2003',
  'The first permanent home of the Lieutenant-Governor of the North-West Territories, built in 1876 and 1877 on a hill above the meeting of the Battle and North Saskatchewan rivers. More than two-thirds of Canada was governed from here from 1878 until 1883, when the capital moved to Regina. In 1883 the building became the first Indian Industrial School in western Canada, where First Nations children were sent as part of the government\'s policy of assimilation; two wings were added in 1889 and it closed in 1914. A Seventh Day Adventist academy followed until 1931, and the Oblates of St. Mary\'s ran a seminary and novitiate from 1932 until 1984. A fire destroyed the building in June 2003.',
  'A three-quarter-hectare hilltop of landscaped grounds with the brick chimney, the foundations and other steadied remains of the fire, and the view over the river valley that the register values. Two modern buildings stand on the parcel too. A provincial heritage property since 1984.',
  'A chimney and foundations on the hill where the Territories were governed, later the site of a residential school.',
  'A provincial heritage property on Government Ridge in Battleford; the landowner and any opening times were not checked. An information board is mapped on Municipal Road 656 at the site (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HP('Government House, Battleford',2916),W('Government House (Battleford)')],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('battleford-town-hall-opera-house','Battleford Town Hall and Opera House','Battleford','sk','civic','standing',52.73787,-108.30482,'area','view','drive','','1912',
  'A red-brick town hall and opera house finished in October 1912 at a cost of $40,000, after council had approved $25,000 the year before. It held the town and rural municipality offices, a Mounted Police post and jail and the library, and its opera house staged concerts, plays, school graduations, dances and firemen\'s banquets. A fire in 1965 damaged the ground floor and closed the opera house; the town hall was repaired and restored in the late 1970s. It is a municipal heritage property.',
  'A two-storey red-brick building with a Classical entablature. The register says it still holds the town office, but not whether the opera house is used; the inside was not checked.',
  'A red-brick prairie town hall with an opera house upstairs.',
  'At 91 24th Street, Battleford; still the town office, according to the register, so its office hours apply; they were not checked. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Town Hall / Opera House',6826)],conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('gardiner-church-battleford','Gardiner Church, Battleford','Battleford','sk','worship','standing',52.7342,-108.30621,'exact','view','drive','','1886',
  'Built in 1886 by the local contractor J. H. Sully for the town\'s Presbyterian congregation from locally fired brick. The register calls it the oldest known masonry Presbyterian church in Saskatchewan. It is named for J. M. Gardiner, who led services here in 1886 and 1887. The congregation merged with the Methodists in 1919 and moved, and the building was then used by groups such as the Royal Canadian Legion and the Boy Scouts, and from 1992 to 2003 held the Saskatchewan Baseball Hall of Fame.',
  'A one-storey Gothic-influenced church of buff-coloured brick, with pointed doors and windows edged in brick, a gable roof with a louvred bell cupola at the front and a decorated brick chimney at the back. Present use and inside not checked.',
  'The oldest brick Presbyterian church in the province, in buff brick.',
  'At 131 20th Street West, Battleford . Present owner and use not checked; look from the street.',
  [HP('Gardiner Church',6824)],conf='mixed',brick='no',bcol='buff',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('almighty-voice-jailhouse-duck-lake','Almighty Voice Jailhouse, Duck Lake','Duck Lake','sk','civic','standing',52.8164,-106.218,'area','open','drive','','1890s',
  'A one-room North West Mounted Police jail from the 1890s where Almighty Voice was held in October 1895 after he killed a cow to feed his hungry family. He escaped, killed a Mounted Police sergeant a week later and was hunted for two years until a two-day fight with about 90 policemen left him and two companions dead, together with two officers and a civilian. The town made the jailhouse a municipal heritage property in 2003.',
  'A small, stucco-covered wooden building with simple log construction and a few barred windows, the makeshift sort of jail the Mounted Police used. It stands on a two-hectare museum site at 5 Anderson Avenue.',
  'The cell where a famous fugitive was held before he escaped.',
  'On a museum site in Duck Lake. The register says the property is a museum site, but the days it opens were not found: ring the museum or the town office before going.',
  [HP('Almighty Voice Jailhouse',1538)],conf='mixed',brick='no',bcol='none',th=None,thn=None,hours='?'),
N('cut-knife-hill-battlefield','Cut Knife Hill battlefield','Cut Knife','sk','fort','marker',52.839,-108.9622,'area','ask','drive','forts','1885',
  'On 2 May 1885 troops under Lieutenant-Colonel William Otter, with artillery, attacked the Cree and Assiniboine camps of Chief Poundmaker at Cut Knife Hill. The attack failed after about six hours, and Otter fell back to Battleford; on Poundmaker\'s orders his column was not pursued. It was part of the 1885 conflict between government forces and Métis and First Nations people over land and treaty rights. The site was named a national historic site in 1923.',
  'No remains of the battle are visible. The register says a plaque stands at the top of a steep hill among rolling grassland and ravines, with the grave of Chief Poundmaker, a monument to Chief Big Bear, a tepee village and a small museum. What is open to visit now was not checked.',
  'A hill where a government attack on Poundmaker\'s camp failed in 1885.',
  'On the Poundmaker Cree Nation reserve, about 16 kilometres from the town of Cut Knife, so it is reserve land: ask the Nation about visiting, and do not enter it without being welcome. Opening times were not found.',
  [HP('Battle of Cut Knife Hill National Historic Site of Canada',18923)],conf='mixed',brick='no',bcol='none',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('prince-albert-heritage-museum','Prince Albert Heritage Museum (the Central Fire Hall)','Prince Albert','sk','civic','standing',53.20607,-105.75467,'area','open','drive','','1911–',
  'The city\'s central fire hall from 1911 until 1976, when it became the Prince Albert Heritage Museum for old objects and memorabilia of the town and its district. The city made it a heritage property in 1981.',
  'A two-storey wood-frame building clad in brick, on a large lot above the North Saskatchewan River, with a symmetrical front, a pediment over the entrance, crescent-shaped windows upstairs under half-moon arches, stone quoins at the corners and a 15-metre hose-drying tower. What the museum shows was not checked.',
  'A 1911 fire hall with a hose tower, now the city museum.',
  'At 10 River Street East, Prince Albert, run as the city\'s heritage museum according to the register; days and hours were not found on a page read for this entry, so ring ahead. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Prince Albert Heritage Museum',4950)],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=None,thn=None,hours='?'),
]
